\tag{fdip}
\title{The Wilson term and other doubling remedies}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{Can the extra zero branches of the centered Dirac operator
be lifted without abandoning a local lattice difference?
The [covariant operator](fdip-009N) supplies a skew-adjoint
first difference. A second difference supplies the Wilson term.
For a positive Wilson coefficient and trivial connection, its
nonnegative scalar Fourier symbol vanishes only at zero momentum.
Adding it to the skew Dirac symbol therefore leaves precisely the
constant zero modes.}

\p{The factor of lattice spacing and the adjoint convention are
part of this statement. Replacing the skew operator by its
self-adjoint multiple #{iD} changes the kernel calculation and
can create extra zeros. \ref{fdip-00LZ} separates that
replacement from the Clifford substitution #{\varepsilon_j^{S}\mapsto\ui\varepsilon_j^{S}}.
The calculation below uses the already specified
reducible Clifford module and internal fiber; it does not identify
one surviving momentum block with a minimal physical spinor.}

\p{The Wilson term also changes the anticommutation relation
with chirality in even dimension. Staggered fields, cochain
constructions and the Ginsparg–Wilson relation address different
parts of that tradeoff. Their comparison here states the carriers
and boundaries of the constructions. The Dirac–Kähler kernels
are the comparison in [Dirac–Kähler operators](fdip-0091).}

